carbon_data = {
    "symbol": "C",
    "name": "Carbon",
    "atomic": 6,
    "category": "polyatomic nonmetal",
    "description": "Chemical element with atomic number 6 (C)",
    "extract_html": "<p><b><a href=\"/school1/ptable/element/C\" class=\"wiki-link\" title=\"Carbon (C)\">Carbon</a></b> (symbol <b>C</b>, atomic number 6) is a versatile polyatomic nonmetal in group 14 of the periodic table. Known since antiquity in forms like charcoal and diamond, carbon is the undisputed chemical backbone of all known life on Earth. Because each carbon atom has four outer electrons, it is \"tetravalent\"—capable of forming four strong covalent bonds with other atoms, including long chains and rings of carbon itself. Carbon makes up about 0.025 percent of Earth's crust. It occurs naturally in three isotopes: stable <sup>12</sup>C (98.93%) and <sup>13</sup>C (1.07%), alongside the radioactive isotope <sup>14</sup>C, which decays with a half-life of about 5,700 years and allows scientists to date ancient organic artifacts.</p>",
    "local_image": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/Pure_Carbon.png",
    "atomic_mass": 12.011,
    "density": 1.821,
    "melt": None,
    "boil": None,
    "phase": "Solid",
    "discovered_by": "Ancient Egypt",
    "electron_configuration": "1s2 2s2 2p2",
    "electronegativity_pauling": 2.55,
    "electron_affinity": 121.7763,
    "ionization_energies": [
        1086.5,
        2352.6,
        4620.5,
        6222.7,
        37831,
        47277
    ],
    "sections": {
        "Characteristics": "</div>\n<figure class=\"mw-default-size mw-halign-left\" typeof=\"mw:File/Thumb\"><img src=\"/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/9a6aeb6acca784b59e2b574971aee376.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"330\" height=\"282\" style=\"--mw-file-upright: 1.3\" class=\"mw-file-element mw-file-upright\" data-file-width=\"2397\" data-file-height=\"2049\" /><figcaption>Phase diagram showing carbon's extreme sublimation point and liquid phase boundaries</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Carbon is famous for taking on astonishingly different physical personalities—called allotropes—depending on how its atoms are arranged. In graphite, carbon atoms bond in flat, hexagonal sheets that easily slide past one another, making it soft, black, and slippery enough to write with. In diamond, carbon atoms form a rigid, three-dimensional lattice where every atom is locked in a tight pyramid structure, creating the hardest naturally occurring substance known. Modern science has unveiled even more exotic carbon structures, such as buckyballs (fullerenes), carbon nanotubes, and one-atom-thick graphene. Renowned Indian scientists, such as Bharat Ratna Prof. C.N.R. Rao, have led pioneering global research into synthesising and understanding these remarkable carbon nanomaterials.</p><p>Chemically, carbon possesses an extraordinary property known as catenation—the ability to form stable, endless chains and rings by bonding with itself and other elements. Because of this, carbon is part of roughly 200 million distinct chemical compounds documented in scientific literature, far surpassing all other elements combined! Carbon also holds the highest sublimation temperature of all elements: at normal sea-level atmospheric pressure, solid carbon does not melt into a liquid when heated. Instead, it turns directly into a gas (sublimes) at an incredible 3,900 K (3,630 °C or 6,560 °F). Liquid carbon is extremely difficult to study because it can only exist under extreme pressures exceeding 100 atmospheres (10.8 megapascals) and temperatures above 4,000 K (3,730 °C). In its gaseous vapor state, excited diatomic carbon molecules (C<sub>2</sub>) produce a striking green glow.</p><p>At the atomic level, carbon is element number 6 with a ground-state electron configuration of 1s<sup>2</sup> 2s<sup>2</sup> 2p<sup>2</sup>, featuring 4 valence electrons in its outer shell. Its first four ionization energies are 1086.5, 2352.6, 4620.5, and 6222.7 kJ/mol—significantly higher than heavier elements in group 14. Carbon has an electronegativity of 2.55 on the Pauling scale, balancing well with neighboring nonmetals. Its covalent bonding radius shrinks as bond strength increases, measuring 77.2 pm for single bonds (C−C), 66.7 pm for double bonds (C=C), and 60.3 pm for triple bonds (C≡C).</p>\n<div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading3\">",
        "Isotopes": "</div>\n<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237\" /><div role=\"note\" class=\"hatnote navigation-not-searchable\">Main article: Isotopes of <a href=\"/school1/ptable/element/C\" class=\"wiki-link\" title=\"Carbon (C)\">carbon</a></div>\n<p>Carbon nuclei always contain 6 protons, but their neutron count can range from 2 to 16 across 15 known isotopes. Earth's natural carbon consists almost entirely of two stable isotopes: carbon-12 (<sup>12</sup>C), which accounts for 98.93%, and carbon-13 (<sup>13</sup>C), which makes up the remaining 1.07%. Biological systems naturally prefer <sup>12</sup>C during photosynthesis and metabolism, leaving living organisms slightly enriched in <sup>12</sup>C. In 1961, the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) designated carbon-12 as the official universal standard for defining atomic weights. Meanwhile, carbon-13 plays a vital role in chemistry and medicine as the active nucleus used in carbon-13 Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) spectroscopy to map organic molecular structures.</p><p>Carbon-14 (<sup>14</sup>C) is a famous natural radioactive isotope produced continuously in Earth's upper atmosphere (between 9 and 15 km altitude). High-energy cosmic rays strike atmospheric atoms, creating thermal neutrons that collide with nitrogen-14 nuclei to yield carbon-14 and a proton. Carbon-14 exists in trace amounts—about 1 part per trillion (1.5 × 10<sup>−10</sup> of atmospheric carbon dioxide). It decays by releasing a 0.158 MeV beta particle (β<sup>−</sup>) with a half-life of 5,700 ± 30 years. Because living organisms absorb carbon dioxide throughout their lives, their carbon-14 ratio matches the atmosphere; after death, <sup>14</sup>C decays predictably without being replenished. Invented in 1949, radiocarbon dating measures this decay to accurately date ancient organic materials, wood, and archaeological relics up to 40,000 years old.</p><p>Among carbon's synthetic isotopes, carbon-8 is the shortest-lived, decaying via proton emission with a fleeting half-life of just 3.5 × 10<sup>−21</sup> seconds. On the exotic extreme, carbon-19 exhibits a loose \"nuclear halo,\" where its outer neutron orbits far outside the central core, giving the atom an unusually large nuclear radius.</p>\n<div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading2\">",
        "Occurrence": "</div>\n<figure class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File/Thumb\"><img src=\"/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/176f8bf3f36d7ce0366de1ebe2fd0ba4.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"250\" height=\"188\" class=\"mw-file-element\" data-file-width=\"600\" data-file-height=\"451\" /><figcaption>Natural graphite ore</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File/Thumb\"><img src=\"/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/d06ef40b45f5946fe7cf3582d5ac905e.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"250\" height=\"188\" class=\"mw-file-element\" data-file-width=\"360\" data-file-height=\"271\" /><figcaption>Raw octahedral diamond crystal</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File/Thumb\"><img src=\"/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/fa3ee3e851d8076d00f381063dc87600.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"250\" height=\"168\" class=\"mw-file-element\" data-file-width=\"1687\" data-file-height=\"1133\" /><figcaption>Global distribution of dissolved inorganic carbon in ocean waters</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Carbon is the fourth most abundant element in the observable universe by mass, trailing only hydrogen, helium, and oxygen. It is abundant in the Sun, distant stars, comets, and planetary atmospheres. Over 20% of all cosmic carbon is bound in complex ring structures called Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons (PAHs). According to the PAH world hypothesis, these space-borne carbon molecules formed roughly two billion years after the Big Bang and may have helped seed the chemical origins of life. Microscopic diamonds also form naturally inside meteorites and at cosmic impact craters, while carbon-rich asteroids in the outer asteroid belt are eyed as potential targets for futuristic space mining.</p><p>Across planet Earth (mass 5.972 × 10<sup>24</sup> kg), carbon averages about 730 parts per million (ppm), with roughly 2,000 ppm locked in Earth's metallic core and 120 ppm spread through the mantle and crust—totalling an estimated 4,360 million gigatonnes (Gt) of carbon. Earth's atmosphere contains around 900 Gt of carbon as carbon dioxide (where 1 ppm equals 2.13 Gt). Since 1750, human burning of fossil fuels has added between 347 and 879 Gt of carbon to the atmosphere, contributing to a total of nearly 2,000 Gt absorbed across the atmosphere, oceans, and land peat bogs. World oceans hold approximately 36,000 Gt of dissolved carbon, while the living biosphere accounts for roughly 550 Gt. Vast icy deposits of methane hydrates beneath polar seabed sediments contain another 500 to 3,000 Gt of carbon.</p><p>Geologically, carbon makes up about 12% of massive carbonate rock formations such as limestone, dolomite, and marble. Fossil fuels represent massive mineralized carbon deposits: global coal reserves contain about 900 Gt of carbon (out of 18,000 Gt of total resources), with high-grade anthracite coal consisting of 92% to 98% pure carbon. Proven crude oil reserves hold about 150 Gt, while natural gas reserves hold 105 Gt (175 × 10<sup>12</sup> m<sup>3</sup>), with an estimated 540 Gt (900 × 10<sup>12</sup> m<sup>3</sup>) trapped in shale gas formations.</p><p>Natural graphite is mined globally in major deposits across China, Russia, Mexico, Canada, and India (with prominent Indian reserves in states like Odisha, Jharkhand, Tamil Nadu, and Arunachal Pradesh). Natural diamonds originate deep within Earth's mantle and reach the surface through ancient volcanic kimberlite pipes found in South Africa, Botswana, Namibia, the DRC, Angola, Brazil, Australia, Russia, Canada, and Arkansas, USA. Historically, India's legendary Golconda mines (in modern Telangana and Andhra Pradesh) were the world's primary source of extraordinary diamonds—including the famous Koh-i-Noor and Hope Diamond. Ancient Indian gemological treatises (<i>Ratnaśāstra</i>) and medieval alchemical texts like the <i>Rasa Ratna Samuccaya</i> by Vāgbhaṭa accurately documented the diamond's unmatched hardness (<i>Vajra</i>) and chemical invulnerability. In modern Indian science, Birbal Sahni (1891–1949), the pioneer of Indian paleobotany, conducted foundational studies on fossilized plant carbon and Indian coal beds, illuminating how ancient plant matter transformed into carbon rich geological formations over millions of years.</p>\n<div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading3\">",
        "Applications": "</div>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r1275594942\">@media all and (max-width:720px){.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery{width:100%!important}}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery{display:table}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery-default{background:transparent;margin-top:4px}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery-center{margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery-left{float:left}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery-right{float:right}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery-none{float:none}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery-center .gallery{justify-content:center}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery-left .gallery{justify-content:left}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery-right .gallery{justify-content:right}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery-collapsible{width:100%}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .title,.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .main,.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .footer{display:table-row}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .title>div{display:table-cell;padding:0 4px 4px;text-align:center;font-weight:bold}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .main>div{display:table-cell}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .gallery.gallery.gallery{line-height:1.35em;display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;column-gap:4px}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .footer>div{display:table-cell;padding:4px;text-align:right;font-size:85%;line-height:1em}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .title>div *,.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .footer>div *{overflow:visible}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .gallerybox img{background:none!important}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .bordered-images .thumb img{outline:solid var(--background-color-neutral,#eaecf0)1px}.mw-parser-output .mod-gallery .skin-invert-image .mod-gallery .whitebg .thumb.thumb.thumb img{outline:solid #d7d7d7 1px}</style><div class=\"mod-gallery mod-gallery-default mod-gallery-center\"><div class=\"title\"><div>Applications of carbon</div></div><div class=\"main\"><div><ul class=\"nochecker whitebg gallery mw-gallery-traditional\">\n\t\t<li class=\"gallerybox\" style=\"width: 145px\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"thumb\" style=\"width: 140px; height: 160px;\"><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><img alt=\"Pencil leads for mechanical pencils are made of graphite\" src=\"/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/2e119d292020ff364ce1864d2207d693.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"110\" height=\"82\" class=\"mw-file-element\" data-file-width=\"785\" data-file-height=\"583\" /></span></div>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"gallerytext\">Graphite pencil leads</div>\n\t\t</li>\n\t\t<li class=\"gallerybox\" style=\"width: 145px\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"thumb\" style=\"width: 140px; height: 160px;\"><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><img alt=\"Sticks of vine and compressed charcoal\" src=\"/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/a2cbd7e8b3a0e48a2daa7cfb8ae551b6.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"110\" height=\"83\" class=\"mw-file-element\" data-file-width=\"1080\" data-file-height=\"810\" /></span></div>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"gallerytext\">Artist's charcoal sticks</div>\n\t\t</li>\n\t\t<li class=\"gallerybox\" style=\"width: 145px\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"thumb\" style=\"width: 140px; height: 160px;\"><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><img alt=\"A cloth of woven carbon fibres\" src=\"/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/8d60fee822e5a6f449c9a54cef949d6a.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"110\" height=\"83\" class=\"mw-file-element\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"768\" /></span></div>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"gallerytext\">Woven carbon fiber cloth</div>\n\t\t</li>\n\t\t<li class=\"gallerybox\" style=\"width: 145px\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"thumb\" style=\"width: 140px; height: 160px;\"><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><img alt=\"Silicon carbide single crystal\" src=\"/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/fdafd43335032f2d16da777461edc423.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"110\" height=\"93\" class=\"mw-file-element\" data-file-width=\"1419\" data-file-height=\"1206\" /></span></div>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"gallerytext\"><a href=\"/school1/ptable/element/Si\" class=\"wiki-link\" title=\"Silicon (Si)\">Silicon</a> carbide crystal</div>\n\t\t</li>\n\t\t<li class=\"gallerybox\" style=\"width: 145px\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"thumb\" style=\"width: 140px; height: 160px;\"><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><img alt=\"The C60 fullerene in crystalline form\" src=\"/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/7dad0cbb26b66ccf0656867d4448bc88.JPG\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"110\" height=\"108\" class=\"mw-file-element\" data-file-width=\"614\" data-file-height=\"601\" /></span></div>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"gallerytext\">Crystalline C<sub>60</sub> fullerene</div>\n\t\t</li>\n\t\t<li class=\"gallerybox\" style=\"width: 145px\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"thumb\" style=\"width: 140px; height: 160px;\"><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><img alt=\"Tungsten carbide endmills\" src=\"/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/87ebdf0a2aed850e97f4306ca08b4be1.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"110\" height=\"46\" class=\"mw-file-element\" data-file-width=\"1200\" data-file-height=\"500\" /></span></div>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"gallerytext\"><a href=\"/school1/ptable/element/W\" class=\"wiki-link\" title=\"Tungsten (W)\">Tungsten</a> carbide endmills</div>\n\t\t</li>\n</ul></div></div></div>\n<p>Carbon is the chemical basis for all known life, forming the structural foundation of proteins, DNA, carbohydrates, and fats. Beyond food and wood, carbon's largest economic role is in energy and materials. Hydrocarbons like natural gas (methane) and crude oil (petroleum) are refined in petrochemical plants to produce gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, and synthetic polymers (plastics). In nature, plants produce cellulose—a natural carbon polymer forming wood, cotton, linen, and hemp—while animals yield natural carbon fibers like wool, silk, and cashmere.</p><p>Carbon is vital across metallurgy and heavy industry. When added to iron, carbon forms steel—the world's premier structural alloy. Historically, ancient Indian metallurgists used charcoal (pure carbon) to smelt iron and forge famous high-carbon Wootz steel (the original Damascus steel), renowned for its strength and wavy patterns. Today, coke (processed coal) acts as a reducing agent in blast furnaces to extract pure iron from ore. Carbides formed with <a href=\"/school1/ptable/element/Si\" class=\"wiki-link\" title=\"Silicon (Si)\">silicon</a>, <a href=\"/school1/ptable/element/W\" class=\"wiki-link\" title=\"Tungsten (W)\">tungsten</a>, <a href=\"/school1/ptable/element/B\" class=\"wiki-link\" title=\"Boron (B)\">boron</a>, and <a href=\"/school1/ptable/element/Ti\" class=\"wiki-link\" title=\"Titanium (Ti)\">titanium</a> are among the hardest materials on Earth, used in industrial cutting, grinding, and metalworking tools.</p><p>Graphite mixed with clay forms pencil leads, while pure graphite serves as a high-temperature lubricant, battery electrode material, motor brush, and neutron moderator in nuclear reactors. Charcoal is widely used in sketching, barbecue grilling, and water filtration. Activated charcoal features a porous surface that traps impurities, making it essential in gas masks, air purifiers, water treatment, and emergency medicine to absorb ingested toxins from the stomach.</p><p>Carbon black—a fine powder of elemental carbon—serves as the dark pigment in printing inks, India ink, oil paints, laser printer toners, and automotive finishes, as well as a reinforcing filler in rubber tires. High-performance carbon fiber, produced by heating synthetic polyacrylonitrile (PAN) or polyester fibers until only carbon atoms remain, creates ultra-strong, lightweight composite materials with a higher tensile strength than steel. Carbon fiber composite structures are critical in aerospace, high-end automotive engineering, and space technology—such as payload fairings and rocket casings engineered by the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) for PSLV and GSLV rockets.</p>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\" class=\"\"></div>\n<div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading3\">"
    }
}

